.TH TULPAR-OWNS 1 "2026" "tulpar 0.7.0" "Tulpar Manual"
.SH NAME
tulpar-owns \- find the package that owns a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tulpar owns
.RI [ options "] " path ...
.br
.B tulpar O
.RI [ options "] " path ...
.br
.B tulpar \-O
.RI [ options "] " path ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Looks up each
.I path
in the package database and prints the package that installed it, one
line per path in the form
.IR path ": " package .
Relative paths are resolved against the current directory. With
.BR \-\-dest ,
a path that starts with the destination root is looked up without that
prefix.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dest " " \fIpath\fR
Operate on the database of an alternate root.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print the result as a JSON array instead of human-readable text. See
.BR tulpar-json (7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every path is owned by a package, 1 if at least one is not or the
database cannot be read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tulpar (1),
.BR tulpar-files (1),
.BR tulpar-why (1),
.BR tulpar-json (7)
